{"ResumeMatch - Resume-to-Job Matching Assistant\n\nNLP-Based Job Fit Scoring System | Use Case Id: UC00208 | T1 2026\n\nStudent: Basil Behanan\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nBackground\n\nApplying for jobs is often inefficient and time-consuming. Candidates\nfrequently apply to roles that do not align well with their skills or\nexperience, leading to low response rates and wasted effort.\n\nThe problem is not a lack of opportunities \u2014 it is a lack of clarity on\njob-fit. Most candidates rely on manual judgment or keyword matching,\nwhich fails when job descriptions use different wording or when the\nrelevance is not obvious.\n\nThere is currently no simple system that can quantify how well a resume\nmatches a job description while also explaining why.\n\nResumeMatch addresses this by combining multiple Natural Language\nProcessing (NLP) techniques to evaluate job-fit in a more structured and\ninterpretable way.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nUse Case\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  Component                               Detail\n  --------------------------------------- -------------------------------\n  Product                                 A Resume-to-Job Matching\n                                          Assistant that generates a Job\n                                          Fit Score based on resume and\n                                          job description alignment\n\n  Primary Actors                          Job seekers, students,\n                                          early-career professionals\n\n  Secondary Actors                        Recruiters, career advisors,\n                                          hiring teams\n\n  Scenario                                A candidate wants to quickly\n                                          determine whether a job is\n                                          worth applying to based on how\n                                          well their resume matches the\n                                          job requirements\n\n  Goal                                    Provide a clear job-fit score\n                                          along with matched and missing\n                                          skills to support better\n                                          application decisions\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nUser Stories\n\n-   As a job seeker, I want to know how well my resume matches a job\n    description, so that I can focus on applying to roles where I have a\n    higher chance of success.\n\n-   As a student or graduate, I want to identify missing skills in job\n    descriptions, so that I can improve my resume and better align with\n    industry expectations.\n\n-   As a career advisor, I want a tool that can explain why a resume is\n    a strong or weak match for a job, so that I can guide candidates\n    more effectively.\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nTechniques Used\n\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n  #    Method             Role in ResumeMatch\n  ---- ------------------ -----------------------------------------------\n  1    TF-IDF + Cosine    Measures keyword-based relevance between resume\n       Similarity         and job description\n\n  2    Semantic           Captures contextual meaning and related\n       Similarity         concepts beyond exact keywords\n       (Sentence          \n       Embeddings)        \n\n  3    Skill Extraction   Identifies technical skills present in resume\n                          and job description\n\n  4    Skill Gap Analysis Highlights matched and missing skills\n\n  5    Hybrid Scoring     Combines multiple signals into a final Job Fit\n       Model              Score\n  -----------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nWorkflow\n\n-   Environment Setup & Data Input \u2014 Load resume and job description\n    (text or PDF)\n-   Text Preprocessing \u2014 Clean and normalize text for analysis\n-   Baseline Matching (TF-IDF) \u2014 Compute keyword similarity score\n-   Skill Extraction & Gap Analysis \u2014 Identify matched and missing\n    skills\n-   Semantic Similarity (Advanced Stage) \u2014 Compute contextual similarity\n    using embeddings\n-   Model Comparison \u2014 Compare TF-IDF vs semantic results\n-   Final Scoring Model \u2014 Combine all signals into a Job Fit Score\n-   UI Application (Streamlit) \u2014 Allow users to upload resume and\n    evaluate job-fit interactively\n\n------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n1) Imports and Setup\n\nLoad required libraries for data handling, NLP preprocessing, matching,\nand visualization.\n\n    import re\n    import string\n\n    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n    import nltk\n    import pandas as pd\n    import requests\n    from nltk.corpus import stopwords\n    from nltk.tokenize import word_tokenize\n    from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer\n    from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity\n\n2) Load Input Data from API\n\nCall API endpoints for the resume text and job postings dataset, then\npreview both. This replaces direct loading from ../data/resume.txt and\n../data/jobs.csv.\n\n    # Replace this with your deployed or local API base URL.\n    API_BASE_URL ": null, " misses jobs using\n    different terminology\n-   Semantic alone: Can overestimate alignment due to general domain\n    similarity without technical depth\n-   Hybrid model: Balances all three signals, producing rankings that\n    account for meaning, skills, and keywords\n\nLimitations & Future Improvements\n\nCurrent Limitations\n\nScoring Weights: The 50/30/20 split is heuristic, not learned from real\nhiring data. Ideally, weights should be calibrated using historical\nhiring outcomes.\n\nSkill List Completeness: The skill extractor depends on a predefined\nlist. It will miss:\n\n-   Domain-specific variations and synonyms (e.g., \"deep neural\n    networks\" vs \"deep learning\")\n-   Industry-specific terminology\n-   Emerging technologies not in the original list\n\nSemantic Model Boundaries: Pre-trained embeddings may overestimate\nrelevance when:\n\n-   Job descriptions are in different domains (e.g., finance vs\n    healthcare)\n-   Technical depth varies significantly\n\nResume-Job Mismatch: The system ranks based on match, but doesn't\naccount for:\n\n-   Seniority alignment (entry-level candidate vs senior role)\n-   Experience depth (breadth vs. specialized expertise)\n-   Company culture fit or growth opportunity\n\nPotential Enhancements\n\n1.  Weight Tuning: Train weights on labeled hiring data (matches vs.\n    non-matches)\n2.  Skill Expansion: Dynamically extract skills using NER (Named Entity\n    Recognition) instead of static lists\n3.  Context Awareness: Weight skill overlap differently based on\n    required vs. nice-to-have skills\n4.  Seniority Detection: Add role-level matching (junior, mid, senior\n    positions)\n5.  Explainability: Generate detailed explanations for each ranking\n    decision\n\nTechnical Reflection & Conclusions\n\nJourney Through Approaches\n\nPhase 1 - Baseline (TF-IDF):\n\n-   Simple and fast, but purely lexical\n-   Fails when job descriptions use different terminology for the same\n    concept\n-   Provides baseline interpretability through term importance\n\nPhase 2 - Semantic Enhancement:\n\n-   Captures conceptual similarity independent of exact wording\n-   More robust to paraphrasing and synonyms\n-   Risk: Over-generalizes (high semantic match \u2260 technical fit)\n\nPhase 3 - Hybrid Integration:\n\n-   Combines precision (TF-IDF), meaning (Semantic), and explainability\n    (Skills)\n-   Demonstrated that no single approach dominates": null, " each adds value\n-   Produced rankings that align with intuitive expectations about role\n    fit\n\nKey Engineering Insights\n\n1.  Signal Complementarity: When three independent signals are combined\n    thoughtfully, the result is more robust than any single method\n\n2.  Weight Ratios Matter: The 50/30/20 split reflects a belief about\n    what matters most in job matching (meaning > skills > keywords).\n    This belief should be validated against real data.\n\n3.  Interpretability is Critical: By including skill overlap scoring, we\n    gain the ability to explain why a job ranked highly (or poorly), not\n    just report a number.\n\nRecommended Next Steps\n\nFor production deployment, this system should:\n\n-   Collect feedback on ranking quality to calibrate weights\n-   Integrate with recruiter feedback loops\n-   Track conversion rates (matches \u2192 applications \u2192 interviews \u2192\n    offers)\n-   Expand skill databases periodically\n\n    # Export final results and comparison artifacts\n    print(\"\\n\" + \"": null}